WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:07.717
Before 1980, Mount St. Helens, in Washington, was just another volcano in the Ring of Fire.

2
00:00:07.718 --> 00:00:12.318
That's the huge circle of volcanoes that ring the Pacific Ocean.

3
00:00:12.319 --> 00:00:16.762
The volcano had not erupted in over a hundred years.

4
00:00:16.763 --> 00:00:20.078
It was green and rich with wildlife.

5
00:00:20.079 --> 00:00:24.940
Spirit Lake, below the mountain, was full of fish and plants.

6
00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:28.600
But then this volcano woke up!

7
00:00:28.601 --> 00:00:34.948
Hot melted rock, called magma, had created lots of gas deep underground.

8
00:00:34.948 --> 00:00:39.464
This gas had built up, causing pressure within the volcano.

9
00:00:39.465 --> 00:00:47.633
On May 18, 1980, an earthquake struck the area—and Mount St. Helens finally erupted.

10
00:00:47.634 --> 00:00:52.778
When the volcano exploded, its entire north side collapsed.

11
00:00:52.779 --> 00:00:56.769
This created the biggest landslide in modern history.

12
00:00:56.770 --> 00:01:02.523
Lava, which is magma that has reached the surface, blasted out of the mountain.

13
00:01:02.524 --> 00:01:06.372
The lava mixed with rock, ash, and melted snow.

14
00:01:06.373 --> 00:01:08.807
It flowed over the landscape.

15
00:01:08.808 --> 00:01:12.566
Clouds of hot gases and rocks burst out too.

16
00:01:12.566 --> 00:01:15.710
They knocked down entire forests.

17
00:01:15.711 --> 00:01:22.565
The violent eruption left a huge crater at the top of the mountain—nearly three kilometers wide.

18
00:01:22.566 --> 00:01:28.536
Mount St. Helens was now four hundred meters shorter than before the eruption!

19
00:01:28.537 --> 00:01:31.652
This wasn't the most serious damage.

20
00:01:31.653 --> 00:01:34.859
Fifty-seven people died in the eruption.

21
00:01:34.859 --> 00:01:39.374
Roads, bridges, and hundreds of homes were destroyed.

22
00:01:39.375 --> 00:01:44.881
Right after the eruption, scientists began to study the scene.

23
00:01:44.882 --> 00:01:48.819
Ash and volcanic rock covered most of the landscape.

24
00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:52.660
Spirit Lake was filled with ash and rock too.

25
00:01:52.661 --> 00:01:56.111
Broken trees covered the surface of the lake.

26
00:01:56.112 --> 00:01:58.819
There was nothing alive in sight.

27
00:01:58.820 --> 00:02:03.000
The entire area looked as lifeless as the moon!

28
00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:09.850
Scientists believed decades would pass before animals and plants returned to the area.

29
00:02:09.851 --> 00:02:15.359
Yet only three months later, scientists spotted something incredible.

30
00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:18.495
A gopher was digging out of its tunnel!

31
00:02:18.496 --> 00:02:23.241
The animal had stayed safe underground during the dangerous eruption.

32
00:02:23.242 --> 00:02:26.501
By the next spring, a plant was growing!

33
00:02:26.502 --> 00:02:31.929
After ten years, plants had grown back and animals had returned.

34
00:02:31.930 --> 00:02:35.606
Plankton and plants filled Spirit Lake again.

35
00:02:35.606 --> 00:02:38.621
Life had returned to the mountain.

36
00:02:38.622 --> 00:02:43.441
Meanwhile the volcano hadn't exactly fallen back to sleep.

37
00:02:43.442 --> 00:02:48.766
After the eruption, lava continued to build up at the crater's floor.

38
00:02:48.766 --> 00:02:51.241
It rose and rose.

39
00:02:51.242 --> 00:02:57.490
By 1986 the lava had formed a dome 270 meters high.

40
00:02:57.491 --> 00:03:01.263
Then the mountain grew mostly quiet again.

41
00:03:01.264 --> 00:03:04.601
Once in a while, the mountain rumbled.

42
00:03:04.601 --> 00:03:07.536
There were even small explosions.

43
00:03:07.536 --> 00:03:11.077
But none of this activity was very serious.

44
00:03:11.078 --> 00:03:16.931
In 2004 ash and steam clouds rose once more from the crater.

45
00:03:16.932 --> 00:03:22.327
Rocks as big as buildings pushed up from the crater floor.

46
00:03:22.327 --> 00:03:24.108
What was happening now?

47
00:03:24.109 --> 00:03:25.719
No one knew.

48
00:03:25.720 --> 00:03:30.167
But by 2007 the mountain went back to sleep.

49
00:03:30.168 --> 00:03:34.793
Scientists think that the volcano may have run out of gas.

50
00:03:34.794 --> 00:03:40.712
Today earthquakes and rumblings continue to occur near Mount St. Helens.

51
00:03:40.713 --> 00:03:45.894
The good news is that scientists have learned a lot since the 1980 eruption.

52
00:03:45.910 --> 00:03:51.337
These lessons will help them to make better predictions about future eruptions.

53
00:03:51.337 --> 00:03:56.026
No one knows exactly when Mount St. Helens will wake up again.

54
00:03:56.026 --> 00:04:04.318
But when it does, people are more likely to be prepared.

